#: ../../source/docs/balance_bot-working.rst:6
#: 1f7573e2c15c4e2b83f3a87e7f47439d
msgid "How Balance Bots work"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/balance_bot-working.rst:8
#: 5d34e20c52134cd5b1640a8a264d36ee
msgid "This section gives a brief insight into how the Balance Bot works. Balance Bot is implemented as a frame class within the Rover firmware."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/balance_bot-working.rst:11
#: d9b0259a823b473a964d1d4e1e282081
msgid "Balancing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/balance_bot-working.rst:15
#: cf11278342524ae59135da5a035c95d3
msgid "A Balance Bot is inherently unstable about its pitch axis. If the vehicle is not perfectly verticle (e.g. pitch angle of zero), gravity applies a torque that causes the vehicle to fall over in the direction of pitch. To counteract this, the motors must apply a reverse torque which causes the vehicle to accelerate in the direction of pitch."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/balance_bot-working.rst:19
#: b3f6df177dc44b229fc2435e7e74b433
msgid "A Pitch PID controller is used to calculate the required throttle to keep the vehicle pitched at a desired angle (e.g. zero to stay balanced without moving forward)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/balance_bot-working.rst:22
#: 007cc2d504cb4621b0e58293b7d2711f
msgid "Accelerate/Decelerate"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/balance_bot-working.rst:26
#: 05886ff2363541ea80a4a98a365988b3
msgid "As explained previously, when the pitch is non zero, the vehicle is forced to accelerate as it tries to keep itself from falling. Taking this idea forward, if the control system is set to keep the vehicle at a non zero pitch, the vehicle will be forced to accelerate continuously as it tries to maintain that pitch. To accelerate forward, the vehicle can be commanded to pitch forward and to decelerate or accelerate backwards, the vehicle can be commanded to pitch back."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/balance_bot-working.rst:30
#: aaf246533bd14c6eb4fe56005a929252
msgid "Pilot Control of a Balance Bot"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/balance_bot-working.rst:32
#: a9365382bf86426689a8188fbf7e3f6c
msgid "With more typical Rovers (e.g. not balance bots), while in Manual mode, the pilot directly controls the vehicle's throttle output using Ch3 of the transmitter (see top section of the image below)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/balance_bot-working.rst:34
#: e8e422280b8043b9a2a1185d6ba7f172
msgid "With Balance Bots however the pilot's throttle controls the desired pitch angle (up to the maximum held in :ref:`BAL_PITCH_MAX<BAL_PITCH_MAX>`).  The throttle output is then calculated by the pitch controller in order to keep the vehicle at the desired pitch."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/balance_bot-working.rst:40
#: 23c71c46731c429abb53788390158af4
msgid "Steering"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/balance_bot-working.rst:41
#: bfff376d90cf43bf995487530beb6de2
msgid "Balance Bots have only two wheels and hence use a skid steering or differential drive system. Steering is done by varying the speed of one wheel relative to the other."
msgstr ""
